# Windows build / package

When the user asks to **build**, **rebuild**, **package**, or fix **missing DLL** errors on Windows, use the project scripts — do not invent ad-hoc `cargo` + manual DLL copy steps.

## Default: Quick

Unless the user explicitly wants a **clean**, **shipping**, **ZIP**, or **full** package:

```powershell
.\scripts\build-release.ps1 -Quick
```

That runs `cargo build --release` (no LTO) and refreshes only `dist\rustcams\rustcams.exe`. It keeps the existing GStreamer plugin/DLL tree and skips the ZIP.

## Full / clean / ZIP

```powershell
.\scripts\build-release.ps1          # cargo --profile dist (fat LTO) + full package + ZIP
.\scripts\build-release.ps1 -Clean   # cargo clean, then dist + full package
.\scripts\build-release.ps1 -SkipZip # full package, no ZIP
```

Use a full (non-Quick) package when `dist\rustcams\` is missing/incomplete, after GStreamer or plugin-allowlist changes, or when producing `dist\rustcams-windows-x64.zip`.

## Cargo profiles (do not “optimize” release with fat LTO)

| Profile | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `release` | Fast day-to-day optimized builds — **no LTO** |
| `dist` | Shipping only — fat LTO + `codegen-units = 1` |

Putting `lto = true` / `codegen-units = 1` back on `[profile.release]` makes every dependency rebuild through linker-plugin LTO and is a major regression.

## What the scripts do

- Load MSVC + GStreamer env automatically
- `-Quick`: `--release` + exe-only package update
- Full: `--profile dist` + wipe/recopy GStreamer DLLs via `package-windows.ps1` + ZIP (unless `-SkipZip`)

## Run the app

Launch **`dist\rustcams\rustcams.exe`** from that folder so bundled DLLs load. Do not run bare `target\release\rustcams.exe` or `target\dist\rustcams.exe` unless GStreamer is already on `PATH`.

## Related scripts

- `scripts/package-windows.ps1` — `-SkipBuild`, `-Quick`, `-CargoProfile release|dist`
- `scripts/package-windows-cross.sh` — Linux cross-build (MinGW), not the default on this Windows machine
